  "statement": {
    "text": "Let $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. Calculate $AB$ and $BA$. Are they equal?"
  },
  "objectives": [
    "Perform matrix multiplication",
    "Understand non-commutativity",
    "Compare products"
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "step-1",
      "order": 1,
      "currentState": {
        "description": "Calculate AB."
      },
      "prompt": {
        "question": "What is $AB$?",
        "hint": "Row × Column: $(AB)_{ij} = \\sum A_{ik}B_{kj}$. First row, first column: $1(0) + 2(1) = 2$"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$AB = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$AB = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$AB = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$"
        }
      ],
      "correctOptionIndex": 0,
      "feedback": {
        "correct": {
          "explanation": "Perfect! Row 1 × Columns: $[1,2]·[0,1]^T = 0+2=2$ and $[1,2]·[1,0]^T = 1+0=1$. Row 2 × Columns: $[3,4]·[0,1]^T = 0+4=4$ and $[3,4]·[1,0]^T = 3+0=3$.",
          "insight": "Matrix multiplication: each entry is dot product of corresponding row from first matrix and column from second."
        },
        "incorrect": {
          "1": "Can't just multiply element-wise! Use row × column dot products.",
          "2": "Check calculations. $(AB)_{11} = 1(0)+2(1) = 2$, not 0."
        }
      },
      "resultState": {
        "description": "Found AB"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step-2",
      "order": 2,
      "currentState": {
        "description": "Calculate BA."
      },
      "prompt": {
        "question": "What is $BA$?",
        "hint": "$(BA)_{11} = 0(1) + 1(3) = 3$, $(BA)_{12} = 0(2) + 1(4) = 4$, etc."
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$BA = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$BA = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ (same as AB)"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$BA = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ (just returns A)"
        }
      ],
      "correctOptionIndex": 0,
      "feedback": {
        "correct": {
          "explanation": "Correct! $B$ row 1 × $A$ columns: $[0,1]·[1,3]^T = 3$, $[0,1]·[2,4]^T = 4$. $B$ row 2 × $A$ columns: $[1,0]·[1,3]^T = 1$, $[1,0]·[2,4]^T = 2$.",
          "insight": "$BA$ swaps the rows of $A$! Matrix $B = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ is a permutation matrix."
        },
        "incorrect": {
          "1": "Order matters! $BA$ is different from $AB$ in general.",
          "2": "Not quite. $B$ as a permutation matrix swaps rows: row 1 becomes row 2, row 2 becomes row 1."
        }
      },
      "resultState": {
        "description": "Found BA"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step-3",
      "order": 3,
      "currentState": {
        "description": "Compare AB and BA."
      },
      "prompt": {
        "question": "Are $AB$ and $BA$ equal?",
        "hint": "$AB = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ but $BA = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}$. They're different!"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "No, $AB \\neq BA$. Matrix multiplication is NOT commutative."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "Yes, $AB = BA$ always"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "They're transposes of each other"
        }
      ],
      "correctOptionIndex": 0,
      "feedback": {
        "correct": {
          "explanation": "Excellent! $AB \\neq BA$. Matrix multiplication is generally NOT commutative. Order matters!",
          "insight": "Important exception: some matrices do commute (e.g., identity matrix, or diagonal matrices with each other). But in general, $AB \\neq BA$."
        },
        "incorrect": {
          "1": "Not in general! We just showed $AB \\neq BA$ for these specific matrices.",
          "2": "Check: $(AB)^T = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\neq BA$. They're not transposes."
        }
      },
      "resultState": {
        "description": "Confirmed non-commutativity"
      }
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "keyInsights": [
      "Matrix multiplication: $(AB)_{ij} = \\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}$ (row × column)",
      "$AB = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ but $BA = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}$",
      "Matrix multiplication is NOT commutative: $AB \\neq BA$ in general",
      "Order matters! Always check which matrix multiplies which",
      "Associative: $(AB)C = A(BC)$, but not commutative: $AB \\neq BA$"
